WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.392 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each thread in this starter doubles two inputs.

00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:04.996 position:50% size:92% align:center
Follow just the first load,

00:00:04.996 --> 00:00:06.659 position:50% size:92% align:center
multiply and store sequence.

00:00:06.879 --> 00:00:09.719 position:50% size:92% align:center
A multiply cannot use its input before that

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:11.503 position:50% size:92% align:center
input arrives. What can the

00:00:11.503 --> 00:00:13.023 position:50% size:92% align:center
SM do during that wait?

00:00:15.043 --> 00:00:18.209 position:50% size:92% align:center
The SM holds state for multiple resident warps.

00:00:18.209 --> 00:00:20.297 position:50% size:92% align:center
Registers hold values belonging

00:00:20.297 --> 00:00:21.443 position:50% size:92% align:center
to their threads.

00:00:21.663 --> 00:00:24.417 position:50% size:92% align:center
Resident means the warp’s state and required

00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:27.233 position:50% size:92% align:center
resources are present on this SM; it does not

00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.236 position:50% size:92% align:center
mean every instruction is ready.

00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:34.277 position:50% size:92% align:center
In this chosen sequence, warp A requests its

00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:37.504 position:50% size:92% align:center
data and then receives it. Its first thread can

00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:39.427 position:50% size:92% align:center
now multiply one point zero.

00:00:39.647 --> 00:00:42.640 position:50% size:92% align:center
The scheduler selects ready instructions; the

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.302 position:50% size:92% align:center
picture is not a measured

00:00:44.302 --> 00:00:46.431 position:50% size:92% align:center
schedule or a fixed GPU latency.

00:00:48.451 --> 00:00:51.638 position:50% size:92% align:center
Warp B now has an outstanding load. Warp A still

00:00:51.638 --> 00:00:54.360 position:50% size:92% align:center
has usable operands in its own registers.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.415 position:50% size:92% align:center
The two warps do not need separate

00:00:56.415 --> 00:00:58.249 position:50% size:92% align:center
arithmetic units to make progress.

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:03.446 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause here. Warp B’s load is pending, but warp

00:01:03.446 --> 00:01:05.517 position:50% size:92% align:center
A’s multiply has its operands.

00:01:05.737 --> 00:01:08.375 position:50% size:92% align:center
Can the scheduler choose A’s ready multiply

00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:10.153 position:50% size:92% align:center
without discarding B’s state?

00:01:15.973 --> 00:01:18.823 position:50% size:92% align:center
Yes. The arithmetic hardware uses A’s ready

00:01:18.823 --> 00:01:21.209 position:50% size:92% align:center
operands and produces two point zero

00:01:21.209 --> 00:01:22.800 position:50% size:92% align:center
for the selected thread.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.808 position:50% size:92% align:center
B’s state remains resident while its load is

00:01:25.808 --> 00:01:28.850 position:50% size:92% align:center
pending. This is hardware sharing between warps,

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.639 position:50% size:92% align:center
not one processor reserved for every thread.

00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:36.595 position:50% size:92% align:center
When B’s values arrive, the same arithmetic

00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:39.601 position:50% size:92% align:center
hardware can use them. Its selected input of

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:41.718 position:50% size:92% align:center
thirty-three point zero becomes

00:01:41.718 --> 00:01:43.152 position:50% size:92% align:center
sixty-six point zero.

00:01:43.372 --> 00:01:45.578 position:50% size:92% align:center
More resident warps can supply ready

00:01:45.578 --> 00:01:48.213 position:50% size:92% align:center
instructions, but dependencies and resource

00:01:48.213 --> 00:01:50.296 position:50% size:92% align:center
limits still matter. This sequence

00:01:50.296 --> 00:01:52.012 position:50% size:92% align:center
does not predict a speed-up.
